"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[3872],{3872:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-09","slug":"base-09","trackId":"base","order":10,"title":"How to Buy Cryptocurrency Safely","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":13,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-08"],"markdown":"Lesson 9 \xb7 9 min\\n\\n## Buying\\n\\nHow to Buy Cryptocurrency Safely\\n\\nThe previous lesson examined Russian crypto regulation, current rules and changes described for coming years. Now consider the practical question:\\n\\nhow can you buy cryptocurrency with ordinary rubles without running into problems?\\n\\nIt looks simple: send rubles and receive USDT or another cryptocurrency. Sending money is usually not the hardest part. The main risks concern:\\n- whom you pay;\\n- where the cryptocurrency originates;\\n- whether it is linked to fraud or sanctioned addresses;\\n- whether an exchange will later accept it;\\n- whether a bank will block a suspicious transfer.\\n\\nLook beyond the exchange rate. Trying to save 0,5% can cause much larger losses.\\n\\n### 1. Ways to Buy Cryptocurrency in Russia\\n\\nAs the previous lesson explained, cryptocurrency is not prohibited in Russia. It can be bought, sold and held subject to applicable law. The source describes regulated crypto-market infrastructure taking shape from September 2026, with Bank of Russia registers covering participants such as conversion services and digital depositories.\\n\\nCheck an organization’s official status directly in Bank of Russia registers. The source describes a transition period through 30 June 2027 with special transaction rules. In practice, purchase methods include:\\n\\nRegulated Intermediaries\\n\\nOrganizations buying and selling cryptocurrency within applicable law.\\n\\nCentralized Cryptocurrency Exchanges\\n\\nPlatforms where users buy, sell and hold cryptocurrency. Funding arrangements and service availability depend on country and exchange rules.\\n\\nP2P Platforms\\n\\nServices through which users buy directly from one another.\\n\\nCrypto Conversion Services\\n\\nServices exchanging ordinary money for cryptocurrency and back. Reliability, legal status and transaction methods vary considerably. Remember:\\n\\na large platform or attractive rate does not guarantee a safe transaction.\\n\\nCheck the company, service availability and the legality of your chosen method before buying. Beginners often overlook another issue.\\n\\n### 2. What Is “Dirty Crypto”?\\n\\nWith an ordinary banknote, you do not know who held it five years ago, where it was used or how often it changed hands. Many blockchains publicly record transfer histories.\\n\\nYou can inspect the addresses through which funds passed before reaching you. Crypto companies may question funds previously linked to:\\n- fraud;\\n- theft;\\n- hacked exchanges;\\n- ransomware;\\n- illegal platforms;\\n- sanctioned addresses.\\n\\nPeople informally call these assets “dirty crypto.” This is not a legal term and does not mean one USDT is physically different from another.\\n\\nIt concerns the history of fund movements and risks associated with particular addresses and operations by screening systems.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\n#### Why This Can Become Your Problem\\n\\nSuppose you buy 1 000 USDT from a stranger, who sends it to your wallet. A month later, you transfer it to a major exchange.\\n\\nThe exchange checks the funds’ history and finds links to addresses previously involved in fraud or suspicious activity. It may request:\\n- proof of the source of funds;\\n- purchase history;\\n- bank documents;\\n- counterparty information;\\n- an explanation of a particular transaction.\\n\\nA transaction or account can sometimes be restricted temporarily during investigation. Suspicious links do not mean you acted unlawfully, but you may have to explain the cryptocurrency’s origin.\\n\\nCheaper is therefore not always better value.\\n\\n### 3. Sanctioned Platforms and AML Checks\\n\\nMajor international exchanges and financial companies consider sanctions imposed by different jurisdictions. Sanctions lists may name specific crypto addresses as well as individuals and organizations.\\n\\nFor example, in August 2025, the US Treasury imposed sanctions on Garantex again and listed Grinex, which US authorities described as its successor. Interacting with certain platforms can therefore cause difficulties when subsequently transferring funds to international exchanges.\\n\\nA service appearing on a foreign sanctions list is distinct from cryptocurrency ownership being prohibited in Russia. International crypto companies may nevertheless restrict operations under their legal obligations and internal rules. Check an unfamiliar platform’s reputation, owners, legal status and possible restrictions.\\n\\n#### What Is AML?\\n\\nAML means Anti-Money Laundering. An AML system assesses how suspicious a crypto transaction history may be. Think of a bank reviewing someone’s credit history to assess risk.\\n\\nAML tools analyze relationships among crypto addresses and transactions, potentially identifying contact with scammers, hacked services or sanctioned organizations. Major exchanges use professional analysis tools, including Chainalysis ↗ (opens in a new tab).\\n\\nSimpler address and transaction screening services also exist for ordinary users.\\n\\n#### When Is an AML Check Useful?\\n\\nIt is particularly useful when you:\\n- buy cryptocurrency from a stranger;\\n- use an unfamiliar conversion service;\\n- receive a large amount from an external wallet;\\n- accept crypto payment;\\n- intend to transfer substantial funds to a centralized exchange.\\n\\nBut remember an important limitation:\\n\\nan AML check does not guarantee completely safe cryptocurrency.\\n\\nServices use different databases, and new suspicious addresses are discovered continuously. Checking a seller’s address beforehand may not identify the history of the particular funds they later send. AML screening reduces risk; it is not absolute protection.\\n\\n### 4. P2P: Convenient, but Understand the Risks\\n\\nP2P means buying cryptocurrency directly from another person. For example:\\n\\nyou want 100 USDT, find a seller, send rubles and receive cryptocurrency. The mechanism is simple, but several risks arise.\\n\\n#### Banking Restrictions\\n\\nA P2P purchase often involves transferring money to another individual. Banks can screen these operations for fraud and anti-money-laundering concerns. A transfer may be suspended while the bank requests documents or explanations. Keep the transaction history and payment confirmation.\\n\\n#### Cryptocurrency Origin\\n\\nConsider where the seller obtained the USDT. An unknown external address may carry historical risks. Counterparty reputation and source-of-funds checks matter.\\n\\n#### Fraud\\n\\nImagine finding a very attractive rate. The seller suggests moving to Telegram and dealing directly, outside the platform. You send rubles; the seller disappears.\\n\\nRecovering the money can be extremely difficult. Choose P2P platforms with transaction protections.\\n\\n#### What Is Escrow?\\n\\nMany P2P platforms use escrow, holding the seller’s cryptocurrency during the transaction. Once the buyer pays and the seller confirms receipt, the crypto is released. If a dispute arises, the platform can examine evidence.\\n\\nEscrow does not protect against every scam. Follow these rules:\\n- Choose counterparties with a long history and many completed trades.\\n- Check ratings and verified identity.\\n- Do not move to outside messaging services to finish the trade.\\n- Refuse payments to third parties or other unusual conditions.\\n- Do not confirm receiving funds until they actually reach your account.\\n\\nThe central P2P rule: do not save a small amount by abandoning protective mechanisms.\\n\\nTap to zoom in\\n\\nBuying USDT through P2P. From whom? Amount: 100 000 ₽.\\n-\\n-\\n-\\n-\\n-\\n-\\n\\n### 5. Internal Transfers Between Exchange Accounts\\n\\nAnother transfer method is worth knowing. Two users may hold accounts on the same centralized exchange. Normally, cryptocurrency can be sent through a blockchain:\\n\\nExchange → blockchain → recipient’s wallet\\n\\nSome exchanges also support direct transfers between users:\\n\\nUser A account → User B account\\n\\nThe exchange changes its internal balance records: one decreases and the other increases, similar to a transfer between customers of the same bank.\\n\\n#### What Is the Advantage?\\n\\nInternal transfers are often faster and may avoid blockchain fees. The user also does not need to send funds to an external address. But remember:\\n\\nan internal transfer does not automatically make cryptocurrency “clean.”\\n\\nThe exchange can still investigate fund origins, request documents or restrict operations. Choose internal transfers for convenience, cost, platform rules and counterparty trust, not because you assume they guarantee freedom from AML checks.\\n\\n### 6. Safely Moving Purchased Cryptocurrency to a Wallet\\n\\nApply what you learned about networks, addresses and transfer fees. Suppose you bought USDT on an exchange and want to withdraw it to your wallet. Check three things.\\n\\n#### 1. Recipient Address\\n\\nEnsure it was copied correctly. Use the copy button, then compare the full pasted address rather than only its beginning and end. Scammers deliberately create similar-looking address prefixes and suffixes.\\n\\n#### 2. Transfer Network\\n\\nUSDT exists on Ethereum ↗ (opens in a new tab), Tron and other blockchains. Verify that the recipient wallet or platform supports your chosen network. An error can make funds inaccessible or require complicated recovery.\\n\\n#### 3. Test Transaction\\n\\nBefore sending a large amount to a new address for the first time, send a small amount. Wait for receipt and verify everything works before transferring the remainder.\\n\\nThis may cost another fee, but a small verification fee is generally preferable to risking the whole transfer.\\n\\nCrypto transactions generally cannot be cancelled by calling support.\\n\\nCheck the details carefully before confirming.\\n\\n### 7. Safe Purchasing Rules\\n\\nBring the lesson together into simple rules.\\n\\n1. Choose a verified platform.\\n\\nConsider reputation, legal status, operating rules and service availability in your country.\\n\\n2. Do not focus only on the rate.\\n\\nA price difference must not outweigh the safety of your money.\\n\\n3. Check the counterparty.\\n\\nThis matters particularly for P2P or conversion services.\\n\\n4. Do not transact outside official protections.\\n\\nUse escrow or other protections offered by the platform.\\n\\n5. Consider the origin of the cryptocurrency.\\n\\nUse available AML screening tools for external addresses, especially for substantial amounts.\\n\\n6. Check the address and network before transferring.\\n\\nSend a small test amount first where appropriate.\\n\\n7. Retain documents.\\n\\nKeep trade histories, bank receipts, statements, purchase confirmations and crypto transfer records. Banks, exchanges or tax authorities may request them.\\n\\n### 8. The Essentials\\n\\nBuying safely is more than finding the best rate. Understand whom you pay, where the cryptocurrency comes from and the reliability of the platform handling the operation. Suspicious history can cause problems after the purchase.\\n\\nAn incorrect address, network or counterparty can cause losses before you even use the funds. Remember:\\n\\nthe goal is not merely to buy cryptocurrency, but to do so without creating subsequent problems with holding, transferring or selling it.\\n\\nYou now know what to check when buying crypto. Buying is only the beginning, however. Many people come to the market to trade rather than merely store money. It initially looks simple: buy cheaper, sell higher and profit.\\n\\nIf it is so simple, why do even experienced participants regularly lose money? Is trading really an easy income source?\\n\\nThe next lesson examines exchanges, why trading is professional territory and the dangers of the easy-money illusion.\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 3\\n\\nWhy should you look beyond an attractive exchange rate when buying USDT?\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Blockchain** — A record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored simultaneously on millions of computers, making falsification of the history physically impossible\\n- **Credit History** — A record of how you have repaid loans. A poor or missing history can mean rejection or an above-market interest rate. No history is worse than a history of repaid loans\\n- **Ethereum** — Not a currency but a platform, a “world computer,” running smart contracts, DeFi and most stablecoins. Second-largest in size and trust\\n- **USDT (Tether)** — The largest stablecoin, supporting almost all crypto turnover. For Russians after 2022, a practical tool for cross-border transfers outside banks\\n- **Regulated Price** — A price set by the government rather than the market: gas, electricity, water and public transport. Changes follow a schedule and a known percentage, allowing households to budget for the increase\\n- **AML Check** — A review of a crypto address’s history for links to fraud, hacks and sanctions. Exchanges perform these checks themselves; they reduce risk but provide no guarantee\\n- **Escrow** — A platform holds the seller’s cryptocurrency until the buyer pays and resolves disputes. It protects you only while the transaction remains inside the platform\\n- **P2P (Peer-to-Peer Transaction)** — Buying cryptocurrency directly from another person through an intermediary platform. It can be cheaper, but the seller, bank and source-of-funds risks remain yours\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: Why should you look beyond an attractive exchange rate when buying USDT?**\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 1: The funds’ origin can cause problems during later exchange screening\\n- ⚪ Option 2: Cheap USDT is always counterfeit\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Every seller must quote the same rate\\n\\n**Question 2: What does “dirty crypto” usually mean?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Tokens that have been physically damaged\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: Cryptocurrency with historical links to fraud, theft, sanctioned addresses or other risky addresses\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Any cryptocurrency bought with a currency other than dollars\\n\\n**Question 3: You are withdrawing a large amount of USDT to your wallet for the first time. What is safest?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Send everything at once to avoid paying two fees\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: Check the address and network, then send a small test transfer first\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Choose any network with a lower fee\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-09-1","prompt":"Why should you look beyond an attractive exchange rate when buying USDT?","options":[{"id":"base-09-1-1","label":"The funds’ origin can cause problems during later exchange screening","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. The funds’ origin can cause problems during later exchange screening"},{"id":"base-09-1-2","label":"Cheap USDT is always counterfeit","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. The funds’ origin can cause problems during later exchange screening"},{"id":"base-09-1-3","label":"Every seller must quote the same rate","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. The funds’ origin can cause problems during later exchange screening"}],"explanation":"The funds’ origin can cause problems during later exchange screening","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:2036–2039 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-09-2","prompt":"What does “dirty crypto” usually mean?","options":[{"id":"base-09-2-1","label":"Tokens that have been physically damaged","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Cryptocurrency with historical links to fraud, theft, sanctioned addresses or other risky addresses"},{"id":"base-09-2-2","label":"Cryptocurrency with historical links to fraud, theft, sanctioned addresses or other risky addresses","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Cryptocurrency with historical links to fraud, theft, sanctioned addresses or other risky addresses"},{"id":"base-09-2-3","label":"Any cryptocurrency bought with a currency other than dollars","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Cryptocurrency with historical links to fraud, theft, sanctioned addresses or other risky addresses"}],"explanation":"Cryptocurrency with historical links to fraud, theft, sanctioned addresses or other risky addresses","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:2041–2044 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-09-3","prompt":"You are withdrawing a large amount of USDT to your wallet for the first time. What is safest?","options":[{"id":"base-09-3-1","label":"Send everything at once to avoid paying two fees","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Check the address and network, then send a small test transfer first"},{"id":"base-09-3-2","label":"Check the address and network, then send a small test transfer first","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Check the address and network, then send a small test transfer first"},{"id":"base-09-3-3","label":"Choose any network with a lower fee","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Check the address and network, then send a small test transfer first"}],"explanation":"Check the address and network, then send a small test transfer first","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:2046–2049 (Verbatim)"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":1774,"lineEnd":2054}}')}}]);